cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "What Your Proxy Cannot See: Measuring Tool-Call Provenance for Autonomous Agents"
message: >-
  If you use this work, cite the archived release rather than the repository's default branch.
  The argument depends on the sealed pre-registration block and on the signed history, and only a
  tagged release fixes both.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Mata García"
    given-names: "Marcos"
    email: "matagarciamarcos@gmail.com"
# Written with the assistance of a coding agent under a written doctrine (CLAUDE.md,
# docs/DOCTRINE.md). 48 of the first 50 commits carry a Co-Authored-By trailer recording it.
# The measurements, the pre-registered thresholds and the disclosure decisions are the author's;
# the agent wrote code and prose against rules fixed before the work started.
repository-code: "https://github.com/marcosmatalab/mcp-fanout"
version: "1.0.0"
# doi: filled in from the Zenodo VERSION DOI after the release is published, in a follow-up
# commit. The version DOI and not the concept one: a citation must resolve to the object the
# reader actually read, and a concept DOI drifts to whatever is newest. The concept DOI lives in
# README.md instead, where drifting to the current version is the desired behaviour.
abstract: >-
  An HTTP proxy selected by the HTTP_PROXY and HTTPS_PROXY environment variables does not observe
  an autonomous agent's outbound traffic. It observes the subset of the agent's clients that chose
  to honour two environment variables, and that subset is not knowable in advance: here it
  silently excluded one of the three components that reached a third party at all, because Node.js
  ignores those variables in its global fetch unless a flag that is off by default is enabled.
  Across ten widely used, version-pinned Model Context Protocol servers, driven under packet and
  capture with 130 tool calls in concurrent waves of 2, 5 and 10 plus a 26-call sequential pass,
  the attributable share of call-caused, content-eligible outbound flows is 0.6579 against a
  threshold of 0.80 sealed by digest before any measurement existed. The instrument does not meet
  its own bar, and every repair to its observability lowered the headline. No fragment of any
  planted context file ever appeared in a request toward a third party. Attribution is shown to
  depend on how many structural tokens a call's arguments commit: over 87 probed tool schemas, 38%
  are attributable from the schema alone, 22% never can be, and 40% are undecided until a value is
  seen.
keywords:
  - autonomous agents
  - Model Context Protocol
  - MCP
  - provenance
  - egress
  - observability
  - supply chain
  - pre-registration
  - negative result
